# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: boethius-1.logic.r1 has "vicious ∨ honest" and "burden → ¬servants_thine" and "¬servants_thine → ¬things_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_counting ∧ ¬things_thine → (beautiful → nothing_to_thee)" and "nothing_to_thee → ¬reason_grieve_joy"; boethius-1.logic.r1.2 has "vicious ∨ honest" and "burden → ¬servants_thine" and "¬servants_thine → ¬things_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_counting → (beautiful → nothing_to_thee)" and "nothing_to_thee → ¬reason_grieve_joy"; they differ when only "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are honest", "the servants (their virtue) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions as really thine", "the things thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions are really thine", "these things are beautiful in their own nature", "thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession" and "they would have been not less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions" are true, which the first allows; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 has "vicious ∨ honest" and "¬servants_thine → ¬things_thine"
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 have "¬reason_grieve_joy"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 has "¬things_thine"; they differ when only "the things thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions are really thine" is true, which the first allows
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 put it at S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 at S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine."
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?", rather than S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine."?
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants (their virtue) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions as really thine"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants (their virtue) can be counted in the sum of thy possessions as really thine"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (they would have been not less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches and it is not so that the things thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions are really thine), then (if these things are beautiful in their own nature, then their beauty is nothing to thee)"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (they would have been not less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches and it is not so that the things thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions are really thine), then (if these things are beautiful in their own nature, then their beauty is nothing to thee)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U3: boethius-1.logic.r1 and boethius-1.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if their beauty is nothing to thee, then it is not so that thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession"; boethius-1.logic.r1.3 does not
